# A dead prefetch worker could take down the boot that only wanted to read its settings (2026-10-05)

`crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/models/startup_prefetch.rs` held 16 of the crate's 39
remaining production `.unwrap()` calls, and all 16 were `std::sync` lock calls on the two guards
this module shares with the thread it spawns. The module is explicitly built to survive a worker
that dies -- `FinishGuard` marks the fetch finished even while the worker unwinds, `State::panicked`
records that it happened, and `accept` logs it and boots without settings -- yet the one failure it
plans for was also the failure that made every later reader of those guards panic. This batch
removes all 16, adds seven tests that hand each guard over poisoned on purpose, and measures the
result with the census and a 14-mutant matrix.

== 0. The module, and the two guards

One fetch of `/v1/models` and `/v1/settings` per process. The begin side is the pager, the commit
side is `bootstrap`; they share no owner object, so the fetch lives in a process global:

    static INFLIGHT: Mutex<Option<Arc<Inflight>>> = Mutex::new(None);

    struct Inflight {
        origin: String,
        state: Mutex<State>,
        done: Condvar,
    }

Every consumer -- `begin`, `begin_before_policy_gate`, `wait_settings`, `accept`,
`accept_with_deadline`, the three `*_for_tests` helpers and `FinishGuard::drop` -- reaches the
result through one of those two mutexes. Each of the 16 calls was the panicking form:

    line  where                                  expression
    46    FinishGuard::drop                      self.0.state.lock().unwrap()
    58    begin_before_policy_gate               INFLIGHT.lock().unwrap().is_some()
    80    begin_inner (cfg!(test) early return)  INFLIGHT.lock().unwrap().is_some()
    82    begin_inner                            INFLIGHT.lock().unwrap().is_some()
    89    begin_inner                            let mut inflight = INFLIGHT.lock().unwrap()
    102   the spawned worker body                worker_cell.state.lock().unwrap()
    119   wait_finished (Condvar argument)       cell.state.lock().unwrap()
    120   wait_finished (wait result)            .wait_timeout_while(..).unwrap()
    131   Finished::take                         INFLIGHT.lock().unwrap()
    136   Finished::take                         std::mem::take(&mut *self.0.state.lock().unwrap())
    143   wait_settings                          INFLIGHT.lock().unwrap().clone()?
    151   wait_settings                          finished.0.state.lock().unwrap().settings.clone()
    169   accept_with_deadline                   INFLIGHT.lock().unwrap().clone()
    218   clear_for_tests                        INFLIGHT.lock().unwrap().take()
    253   inject_with_origin_for_tests           *INFLIGHT.lock().unwrap() = Some(cell)
    259   inflight_for_tests                     INFLIGHT.lock().unwrap().is_some()

Line numbers are the ones the census reported before the change; section 3 has the command that
lists them and the clone it was run against.

== 1. How a guard here gets poisoned, and what the panic then costs

The registry guard is the one with a concrete mechanism on the shipped path. `begin_inner` takes
`INFLIGHT`, allocates the `Inflight`, spawns the worker and only then stores the cell, so the
`std::thread::spawn` that can panic on resource exhaustion runs inside the critical section:

    let mut inflight = INFLIGHT.lock().unwrap();
    if inflight.is_some() {
        return true;
    }
    let cell = Arc::new(Inflight { .. });
    let worker_cell = cell.clone();
    std::thread::spawn(move || { .. });      // panics if the OS refuses the thread
    *inflight = Some(cell);

A refused thread at boot is exactly the situation this code is written for -- the boot should
continue with no settings -- and instead it poisoned the global registry. From that point every
`begin*`, `wait_settings`, `accept`, `clear_for_tests` and `inflight_for_tests` call in the process
panicked with "already poisoned" instead of reporting "no fetch in flight".

The state guard's cost is the worse of the two and does not need a poisoned guard to be worth
fixing: `FinishGuard::drop` takes it, and that `Drop` runs on the worker's way out, including while
the worker is already unwinding. A panic raised inside a `Drop` that is already unwinding aborts
the process, so the guard read in `FinishGuard::drop` is the one line in this file where the
panicking form turns a lost fetch into a dead binary.

Both guarded values stay structurally valid across a panic -- `State`'s fields are plain `Option`s
and the registry is an `Option<Arc<_>>` -- so reading them is safe. What the panicking form bought
was nothing: there is no invariant here that a poisoned guard protects.

== 2. The change

One module-private helper, and 14 substitutions covering the 16 expressions (line 119/120 is one
call whose result is unwrapped on the next line, and the three `cfg!(test)` early returns share a
shape with the real ones):

    /// Locks a guard of this module without panicking on poisoning.
    ///
    /// A poisoned guard here means the prefetch worker died while holding it, which
    /// is the one failure this module is built to survive: [`State::panicked`]
    /// records it and every consumer is written to cope with a dead worker. Both
    /// guarded values stay structurally valid across a panic -- `State`'s fields are
    /// plain `Option`s and the registry is an `Option<Arc<_>>` -- so the data is
    /// readable as it stands. An `unwrap` would instead turn one dead worker into a
    /// second panic in whatever thread only wanted to read settings, and in
    /// [`FinishGuard::drop`] it would be worse: panicking while already unwinding
    /// aborts the process.
    fn lock_or_recover<T>(guard: &Mutex<T>) -> MutexGuard<'_, T> {
        guard.lock().unwrap_or_else(PoisonError::into_inner)
    }

The file now contains no `.unwrap()` at all, production or test, and the `Condvar` result uses the
same recovery (`.wait_timeout_while` returns `LockResult`, so the poisoning arrives there as an
`Err` rather than as a panic):

    $ grep -c '\.unwrap()' crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/models/startup_prefetch.rs
    0

`unwrap_or_else(PoisonError::into_inner)` recovers the data without clearing the poisoning: the
mutex stays poisoned, so a `.unwrap()` elsewhere still reports that a holder died. The test suite
relies on that and pins it explicitly (section 4).

