== 4. The tests

Seven new tests, all driving shipped functions rather than the helper. Each poisons a guard the way
the real failure does -- a thread dies holding it, the panic caught one frame above the guard, and
the thread scope's join makes the poisoning visible before the test proceeds:

    fn poison_through_a_panicking_thread<T: Send>(guard: &Mutex<T>) {
        std::thread::scope(|scope| {
            scope.spawn(|| {
                let _ = std::panic::catch_unwind(std::panic::AssertUnwindSafe(|| {
                    let _held = guard.lock().unwrap();
                    panic!("a worker dying inside the guard");
                }));
            });
        });
        assert!(guard.is_poisoned(), "the poisoning above must be observable");
    }

    cargo test -q -p xai-grok-shell --lib agent::models::startup_prefetch

    running 11 tests
    ...........
    test result: ok. 11 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 6904 filtered out; finished in 0.05s

    1  begin_does_not_replace_an_inflight_fetch                    (pre-existing)
    2  accept_discards_a_fetch_from_another_origin                 (pre-existing)
    3  accept_deadline_spends_the_budget                           (pre-existing)
    4  wait_settings_leaves_the_fetch_for_accept                   (pre-existing)
    5  the_finish_guard_finishes_the_fetch_through_a_poisoned_state_guard
    6  wait_finished_yields_a_poisoned_guard_instead_of_reading_as_a_timeout
    7  a_poisoned_guard_under_a_sleeping_waiter_ends_the_wait_without_a_panic
    8  accept_consumes_a_fetch_behind_a_poisoned_registry_guard
    9  accept_consumes_a_fetch_behind_a_poisoned_state_guard
    10 wait_settings_finds_the_fetch_through_a_poisoned_registry_guard
    11 begin_reports_the_in_flight_fetch_through_a_poisoned_registry_guard

Test 6 is the one that keeps `wait_finished` honest in both directions: a poisoned guard on a
finished fetch must read as "the worker is done", not as a deadline, or the boot would silently
throw away settings that were waiting there. Test 7 is its mirror -- the worker died without
notifying, so the wait must spend its budget and return, not panic.

Test 8 and 10 derive their expectation from the same knob the shipped code reads,
`resolve_remote_fetch_enabled()`, instead of skipping when the egress gate is closed. That gate is
config-on-disk with no environment override and defaults to off, so on this host and in CI a test
that wanted "settings handed out" would have had to skip; both tests assert whatever the shipped
gate decides, and test 8 then adds the assertions that hold either way -- the consumed fetch must
be deregistered, and a later `inject` / `clear` must still work through the poisoned guard. Test 9
is the same shape for the second guard, and asserts the deregistration rather than the returned
settings, because `Finished::take` runs before the gate is consulted.

The recovery deliberately does not clear the poisoning, and one test pins that too, so nobody can
later "tidy" it into `clear_poison()` and lose the signal for other modules:

    assert!(
        guard.is_poisoned(),
        "recovery must not silently clear the poisoning: another module's \
         `.unwrap()` still has to see that a holder died"
    );

== 5. Mutation matrix

`{SCRATCH}/mutate.py` puts one site back to the panicking form, runs this module's tests, records
which tests noticed, then restores the file and verifies the restore with `cmp`; a mutant that
cannot be reverted aborts the run. Two runs were needed and the reason is section 6.

    $ cd {SCRATCH} && python3 mutate.py; echo "matrix exit=$?"
    M1  FinishGuard::drop guard: caught (1 failing tests)
    M2  begin_before_policy_gate pre-gate read: caught (3 failing tests)
    M3  wait_finished state lock: caught (3 failing tests)
    M4  wait_finished wait result: caught (1 failing tests)
    M5  Finished::take registry: caught (3 failing tests)
    M6  Finished::take state: caught (1 failing tests)
    M7  wait_settings registry read: caught (1 failing tests)
    M8  wait_settings state read: MISSED (0 failing tests)
    M9  accept_with_deadline registry read: caught (2 failing tests)
    M10 clear_for_tests: caught (4 failing tests)
    M11 inject_with_origin_for_tests: caught (2 failing tests)
    M12 inflight_for_tests: caught (4 failing tests)
    M13 the shared helper itself: caught (9 failing tests)
    M14 the three sites under `if cfg!(test)`: MISSED (0 failing tests)
    12 caught, 2 survived, 0 contrary to expectation
    matrix exit=0

    $ cmp {SCRATCH}/startup_prefetch.rs.pristine \
          crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/models/startup_prefetch.rs && echo RESTORED
    RESTORED

M13 kills 9 of the 11 tests at once, which is the point of the shared helper: one mutant, and the
whole module's coverage reacts.

The two survivors are not gaps in the tests; no unit-test build of this crate can reach either site:

* M8, the guarded read on the last line of `wait_settings`. It sits behind `still_accepted`, which
  reads `resolve_remote_fetch_enabled()` from config on disk (default off, deliberately no
  environment override, and a unit test may not rewrite the real `$HOME`). With the egress gate
  closed -- the state on this host and in CI -- the call returns before reaching that line. The
  integration binaries under `crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/tests/` do open the gate (their helper
  writes `remote_fetch = true` into an isolated home) and drive the real path, but they cannot
  poison a private `Mutex` field from outside the module.
* M14, the three sites under `if cfg!(test) { ... }`. A unit-test build takes the early return, so
  the non-test form is not compiled into the test binary at all. Those three lines are the shape
  the shipped build uses and are covered by the same integration binaries.

Recording a survivor as "structurally unreachable here" is only honest while the reason holds. If
the egress gate ever gains a test seam, M8 stops being excusable and the test for it is missing.

== 6. The first run found a hole in the tests

The first matrix run had M6 (the guarded read inside `Finished::take`) as a third survivor, and the
run reported it as contrary to expectation:

    M6  Finished::take state: MISSED (0 failing tests)
    ...
    11 caught, 3 survived, 1 contrary to expectation
    matrix exit=1

The existing tests poisoned the registry (`INFLIGHT`) but never the per-fetch `state` guard on the
way into `accept`, so nothing read that line with the guard poisoned. Adding test 9 closed it: the
second run reports M6 caught by one test, which is the tightest possible signal -- one site, one
test that reads it. The first run's logs are kept next to the second's
(`{SCRATCH}/mutation-run1.log`, `{SCRATCH}/matrix-run1.stdout`) rather than overwritten, because
the hole and its closure are the useful part.

== 7. Behaviour change, stated plainly

Nothing about a healthy fetch changed: the same values are read, in the same order, under the same
locks. What changed is what a poisoning panic used to do. A boot that would previously have
panicked -- in the pager reading settings, in `bootstrap` consuming the fetch, or in the process
aborting through `FinishGuard::drop` -- now reads whatever the dead holder left, and the module's
existing reporting takes over: `State::panicked` logs "settings prefetch thread panicked", the
fetch is consumed or left registered exactly as before, and the boot continues without settings.

== 8. Gates and reproduce

The host runner refuses to call itself green while Rust files are changed and its build gates
were skipped, so the plain run reported the change instead of a verdict and exited non-zero:

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh
    ...
    SKIP  cargo check (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  cargo clippy (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  cargo test (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  GUI protocol types (build gate; --with-build runs it)

    NOT COVERED  4 build gate(s) skipped while 2 changed file(s) are theirs to measure:
                  crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/models/startup_prefetch.rs
                  crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/models/startup_prefetch_tests.rs
                  run: scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build

That refusal is the runner working as designed on a Rust batch. With the build gates included, the
same tree passes everything it runs:

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build
    ...
    PASS  cargo check
    PASS  cargo clippy
    PASS  cargo test
    PASS  GUI protocol types

    all gates passed on the host (42 run, 0 skipped)

    $ cargo test -q -p xai-grok-shell --lib agent::models::startup_prefetch
    test result: ok. 11 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 6904 filtered out; finished in 0.05s

To reproduce the matrix from a clean checkout of this commit, with the module's sources saved
aside first:

    $ cd <this repo>
    $ cargo test -q -p xai-grok-shell --lib agent::models::startup_prefetch   # 11 passed
    $ python3 scripts/ci/panic-site-census.py --check-baseline scripts/ci/panic-site-baseline.tsv
    baseline holds: 97 crates, 0 fewer production sites than recorded

and then, for one site at a time, replace `lock_or_recover(&X)` with `X.lock().unwrap()` and re-run
the test command: every site except the two named in section 5 must turn it red.
